<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel García Granados</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel García Granados (1809–1878) was a Guatemalan soldier and politician who led the Liberal Revolution of 1871 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a>, overthrowing the conservative government of Vicente Cerna. As provisional president from 1871 to 1873 he expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and began liberal reforms against the power of the church, before handing power to Barrios.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados.jpg" alt="Miguel García Granados" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joaquín Méndez; Gerardo Garrido Taboada; Felipe Estrada Paniagua; Alberto G. Valdeavellano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Locomotor_1906_Garc%C3%ADa_Granados_(HQ)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>García Granados was born on 29 September 1809 in El Puerto de Santa María, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, into a wealthy military family, and was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> as a young adolescent. At twenty-three he travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, and he completed his schooling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His sister was the Guatemalan writer María Josefa García Granados. He became known as a moderate liberal who compromised with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> and tried to get along with his successor, Vicente Cerna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Sympathising with the revolt against the government, he went into exile with the support of Guatemalan liberals, and on his return he became the leader of the revolution against Cerna and came to be known as its philosopher. As provisional president after the liberal victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> he sought to regularise the government by building a lawful regime, decreed freedom of the press and supported liberal revolts elsewhere.</p><p>He created the Guatemalan flag by decree, and today's flag is almost identical to his version of 1871. He also supported education and the arts, inviting the Italian conductor Pietro Visoni in 1871 to direct the main military band; Visoni went on to found the Martial Symphony Band and the first formal music conservatory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After stepping down he wrote for Guatemalan magazines and newspapers, producing accounts of his years as liberal leader and president, an essay on monetary policy and two volumes of memoirs. In 1877 the Cuban writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-marti">José Martí</a>, teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, often played chess with him and paid his respects to his daughter María García Granados y Saborío, who fell in love with him; Martí returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1878 already married, and María died a few weeks after he left. Her father died soon after, on 8 September 1878, and was buried in the old San Juan de Dios Cemetery. In 1894 his remains were moved to a monument at the new General Cemetery, and his portrait appears on the 10 quetzal note.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel García Granados” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Liberal Revolution of 1871, led by <b>Miguel García Granados</b> and Justo Rufino Barrios, overthrew Carrera’s successor Vicente Cerna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in El Puerto de Santa María, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.6,-6.24&amp;year=1853" aria-label="El Puerto de Santa María on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4393 844 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miguel García Granados"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4843 1114h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4843 1114h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.6,-6.24&amp;year=1853">What was here in 1853? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
